<h2>A victim\'s SLP against sentencing leniency</h2>
<p>Most special leave petitions in criminal matters are filed by convicts. This one travelled the other way: the informant, brother of the injured victim, challenged not the conviction — which the High Court had affirmed — but the sentence that followed it. Article 136 jurisprudence has long recognised the victim\'s standing to question manifestly inadequate sentencing, and the present case shows the jurisdiction operating at full strength: leave granted, the probation order dissected against the statutory preconditions, and the matter restored for a lawful disposal.</p>

<h2>What Section 4 of the Probation Act actually requires</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Eligibility</strong><p>The offence must not be punishable with death or imprisonment for life, and the court must regard the circumstances, the nature of the offence and the character of the offender.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Information</strong><p>The Probation Officer\'s report, where obtained, must actually be considered — Section 4 contemplates an informed discretion, not a benevolent reflex. Here the report existed and went unexamined.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Character</strong><p>Good conduct is the premise of release on good-conduct bonds. Multiple pending criminal cases and a proposed preventive-law action against the convicts negated that premise entirely.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The gravity mismatch</h2>
<p>The Court also addressed the offence itself. The injuries were grievous, inflicted with a cutting weapon and a firearm, and the Bench observed that the facts would more appropriately have attracted Section 307 IPC — attempt to murder — though, the charge having been framed under Section 308 and the trial concluded, little could be done about the framing at this distance. The observation nonetheless did real work: it framed the probation order as leniency stacked upon an already lenient charge, in a case where the victim\'s injuries were permanent. Sentencing, the judgment reminds appellate courts, must answer the crime proved, not the convenience of closure.</p>

<h2>The procedural outcome — and a caution</h2>
<p>The operative directions are strict: surrender within two weeks, apprehension by the Sessions Court in default, and restoration of the criminal appeals before the Lucknow Bench to be heard only after custody resumes. For practitioners, the decision is a caution on both sides of the appellate bench. Defence counsel seeking probation must build an affirmative record of character — antecedent verification, the Probation Officer\'s report, employment and family circumstances — because unexamined leniency is now reversible at a victim\'s instance. Prosecution and complainant counsel, conversely, should place pending-case records and preventive-action proposals on the appellate record explicitly, since it was precisely that material which unravelled the release here.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>For offences committed on or after 1 July 2024, Section 308 IPC corresponds to Section 110 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, and Section 307 IPC to Section 109. The Probation of Offenders Act, 1958 continues to apply alongside the new codes, and Sections 360-361 CrPC find their successors in Section 401 of the BNSS.</p>
</div>
